const GUIDES = {
  "supabase-to-neon": "# Supabase → Neon: estimate cost & assess migration (agent playbook)\n\nYou reached this by fetching the neon-estimator API. You are an agent helping a user decide whether to\nmove a **Supabase** project to **Neon**, and what it would cost. This document is the playbook — follow\nit end to end. No install required.\n\n## How to use this endpoint (the loop)\n\n1. **Read this playbook.**\n2. **Extract** the workload from the user's *live* Supabase project — **read-only** (Step 1). Use\n   whatever access the user has: the Supabase MCP server, the Supabase CLI, a direct Postgres\n   connection, or the Management/Metrics API.\n3. **POST** what you gathered to `POST /api/estimate` (same origin as this guide) to get the Neon cost +\n   range (Step 2). The server does the CU mapping; you don't have to.\n4. **Assess** feature-fit (Step 3) and the migration path (Step 4), then **report** cost diff +\n   feature gaps + migration effort. Honor the guardrail at the end.\n\n**Everything you run against Supabase is READ-ONLY** — never change compute tier, write data, or call\ncost-*changing* endpoints; if a surface is inaccessible, fall back or ask rather than guess on the inputs\nthat move the answer.\n\n**What may leave the user's environment — and what must not.** A server-side estimate needs only a small\nset of **aggregate, non-sensitive numbers**: instance size, DB size in GB, avg CPU %, active hours,\negress GB (or a `workload` of those). **Never send** credentials, connection strings, API keys,\n`service_role`/JWT secrets, personal or user data (emails, names, `auth.users` rows), actual table rows,\nor schema/data dumps — **counts and sizes only**. **Before sending anything off the machine, show the\nuser the exact JSON you will POST and get their confirmation.** The endpoint is a first-party Neon\nestimator that only prices those numbers and returns the result — it is **stateless and stores nothing**.\n\n**Prefer to send nothing at all?** You don't have to POST. This guide's **\"Compute locally (no POST)\"**\nsection (end of Step 2) gives the exact rates, conversions, and formulas to compute the estimate\nyourself — then **no data leaves the user's environment**.\n\nWhy this isn't just copying a number: Supabase bills a **fixed instance 24/7**; Neon **autoscales and\nscales to zero after ~5 min idle**. So you must infer *average* utilization, *peak* capacity, and\n*active hours* — that gap is where the Neon saving (or lack of one) comes from.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Extract the workload (read-only)\n\nGather these five fields. The **[BEST]** surface is listed first; fall back down the list. Note a\n`confidence` and `source` for each — the estimator widens its range on low-confidence inputs.\n\n### `storageGb` — database size — **high confidence**\n- **[BEST] Direct SQL:** `SELECT pg_database_size(current_database());` → bytes ÷ 1e9.\n  (Supabase MCP: `execute_sql`. CLI: `supabase inspect db db-stats --linked` for DB totals, or run the\n  SQL via `supabase db query --linked` (CLI ≥ 2.79) or psql on the **unpooled** connection. There is no\n  `supabase db size` command.)\n- File Storage (buckets) is *separate* — size it under Feature-fit (Step 3), not here.\n\n### instance tier → `peakCu` (and the vCPU for `avgCu`) — **high confidence for the tier**\n- **[BEST] Management API:** `GET https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/{ref}/billing/addons`\n  (`Authorization: Bearer <personal-access-token>`) → the **active compute instance size**. Authoritative.\n- ⚠️ **Not** reliably in the **CLI** (`projects list` has no compute column) or **MCP** `get_project`\n  (status/region only). Ignore `get_cost`/`confirm_cost` — those are for *creating* projects.\n- Map tier → vCPU/RAM (table below), then `peakCu = max(vCPU, RAM/4)`, capped at **16** (Neon's\n  autoscaling ceiling — Free up to 2 CU, **Launch and Scale both autoscale up to 16 CU**; Scale also\n  offers fixed sizes to 56 CU). A CU covers both vCPU and RAM, so peak clears whichever binds.\n\n**Supabase compute tiers** (`supabase.com/docs/guides/platform/compute-and-disk`):\n\n| Tier | vCPU | RAM | Class |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nano (free) | ~0.5 | 0.5 GB | shared / burstable |\n| Micro | 2 | 1 GB | shared / burstable |\n| Small | 2 | 2 GB | shared / burstable |\n| Medium | 2 | 4 GB | shared / burstable |\n| Large | 2 | 8 GB | dedicated (bursts) |\n| XL | 4 | 16 GB | dedicated |\n| 2XL | 8 | 32 GB | dedicated |\n| 4XL | 16 | 64 GB | dedicated |\n| 8XL | 32 | 128 GB | dedicated |\n| 12XL | 48 | 192 GB | dedicated |\n| 16XL | 64 | 256 GB | dedicated |\n\n### `avgCu` — the billed compute (the crux) — **medium/low confidence**\n- **CPU source (verified against Supabase docs):** the **project Metrics endpoint** —\n  `GET https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/customer/v1/privileged/metrics` — HTTP **Basic auth**, username\n  `service_role`, password = a `sb_secret_…` key (preferred, rotatable) or the service_role JWT. Returns\n  ~200 Prometheus series. **Gated to Pro/Team/Enterprise, Beta, not on Free or self-hosted.** (Alias with\n  a Personal-Access-Token bearer: `GET https://api.supabase.com/v1/projects/{ref}/analytics/endpoints/project.metrics`,\n  120 req/min — same data.)\n  - CPU%: from `node_cpu_seconds_total` → `100 - avg(rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode=\"idle\"}[5m]))*100`.\n    Memory: `node_memory_*`. Network out: `node_network_transmit_bytes_total`.\n- ⚠️ **The endpoint has NO history — it's a live snapshot refreshed every ~60 s.** A one-shot read gives\n  you **\"right now,\" not a 7–30 day hourly profile.** So in a normal agent run you usually get a\n  **point-in-time** CPU% (mark it low-confidence — it may not represent the daily average). You only get\n  a real hourly series if the user *already* feeds a Prometheus/Grafana/Datadog store you can query, or\n  from the Supabase **Dashboard → Reports** page (which shows hourly CPU/memory averages, but as a UI —\n  not a documented read API). If you have a genuine hourly series, send it as `profile` (Step 2).\n- **Formula (single average / snapshot):** `avgCu = clamp(vCPU × avgCPU%, 0.25, peakCu)`. CPU% is a\n  per-vCPU measure — multiply by vCPU, not by the RAM-sized peak.\n- **On Free / self-hosted / DB-creds-only:** the Metrics endpoint is unavailable → fall back to\n  in-database sampling (`pg_stat_statements.total_exec_time` deltas, `pg_stat_database`, connection\n  counts) as a *coarse* CPU proxy, and lean on the two-question rule. Label CPU% an estimate.\n- ⚠️ **Shared/burstable tiers (Nano–Medium):** the \"2 cores\" is a *burst* ceiling, not sustained vCPU.\n  Use *measured* CPU% here; if only nominal specs exist, treat sustained vCPU fractionally (≈0.25×) and\n  flag the estimate low-confidence.\n- ⚠️ **RAM working set is a floor.** If the buffer-cache hit ratio is low, the CPU-driven avg understates\n  real CU: `SELECT sum(blks_hit)::float/NULLIF(sum(blks_hit)+sum(blks_read),0) FROM pg_stat_database;`\n  Consistently < ~0.99 ⇒ working set exceeds RAM ⇒ raise the avg/peak floor and say so.\n\n### `activeHours` — the scale-to-zero signal (highest value, softest) — **medium/low**\n- Supabase bills 24/7 regardless; Neon scales to zero, so the hours the DB is *actually active* drive the\n  Neon bill — often the single biggest lever.\n- **[BEST] Activity from the CPU/connection time-series** (hours with non-idle backends or CPU above an\n  idle floor; ignore idle windows > 5 min — that's when Neon suspends).\n  - Point-in-time SQL fallback (sample repeatedly): `SELECT state, count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity GROUP BY state;`\n- Categories as a last resort: always-on = 744 h; business-hours ≈ 264 h; intermittent/dev ≈ 15%.\n- ⚠️ Pooling / idle-in-transaction / `pg_cron` keeps compute warm and defeats scale-to-zero. If a\n  Supavisor pool holds active connections or `pg_cron` runs often, don't claim the idle win.\n\n### `egressGb` — outbound transfer — **high (from billing) / low (inferred)**\n- **[BEST] Supabase Billing → Total Egress** (unified across DB/Storage/Realtime/Auth/Functions), with\n  per-day / per-service breakdown. Egress is not a clean single SQL metric.\n- If unreadable, **ask** — never silently assume 0 (it usually understates).\n\n---\n\n## Step 2 — Get the Neon estimate\n\nTwo ways: **(A) POST** the aggregate numbers and let the server price them (canonical result + range +\nvalidation), or **(B) compute locally** from the rates/formulas below and POST nothing. Both give the\nsame numbers; B keeps all data on the user's machine.\n\n> **Before you POST (option A):** show the user the exact request body and get their OK. It's a handful of\n> **aggregate numbers** (sizes, CPU %, hours) — **no credentials, no PII, no rows**. The endpoint is a\n> first-party Neon estimator that prices those numbers and returns the result; it stores nothing.\n\n### A) POST to the API\n\nAll paths below are on the **same origin as this guide**.\n\n**Preferred — let the server map the signals** (it echoes the extracted workload + confidence so you can\nshow your work):\n\n```\nPOST /api/estimate\n{\n  \"supabase\": {\n    \"instance\": \"large\",        // tier key from the table (lowercase; \"2xl\",\"4xl\",…)\n    \"dbSizeGb\": 8,              // pg_database_size / 1e9\n    \"avgCpuPct\": 15,           // measured avg CPU % (omit → assumes 50% of peak, low confidence)\n    \"activity\": \"always_on\",   // always_on | business_hours | intermittent | unknown\n    \"egressGb\": 40             // from Billing (omit if truly unknown, then ASK)\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**If you have an hourly CPU series** (best accuracy), price it via the profile integrator — same endpoint:\n\n```\nPOST /api/estimate\n{ \"profile\": [ /* 744 hourly CPU% values */ ], \"instance\": { \"vcpu\": 2, \"maxCu\": 8 }, \"storageGb\": 8, \"egressGb\": 40 }\n```\n\n**Advanced — send the workload yourself** (each field `{value, confidence, source}`, or a bare number):\n`POST /api/estimate {\"workload\": {\"peakCu\": 2, \"avgCu\": 0.3, \"activeHours\": 744, \"storageGb\": 8, \"egressGb\": 40}}`.\n\n> One endpoint: **everything goes to `/api/estimate`** — the server routes on the payload shape. A\n> Supabase migration uses `{supabase}` (or a `{workload}`); the measured shapes (`{cuHours}`,\n> `{snapshot}`, `{profile,instance}`) are for someone already on Neon.\n\nThe response gives Launch/Scale monthly totals, a **range**, the **top movers** (which inputs most move\nthe price), Free-tier fit, and a factual \"why Neon\" narrative. It also returns:\n- **`recommendation`** — the headline: `{plan, monthlyTotal, note}`. **If the workload fits Free, this\n  says Free ($0)** — report that, not the Launch price; Launch/Scale are then only for their features.\n- **`assumptions`** — the estimate presupposes **autoscaling + scale-to-zero are enabled** (that's the\n  entire source of the saving). State this when you report; a fixed Neon compute has no cost edge.\n- **`withoutAutoscaling`** — the fixed-compute (peak × 744 h) baseline. The gap between it and the\n  estimate *is* the autoscaling saving — show both so the user sees where the win comes from.\n- **`validation`** — `{verdict: ok|suspect, warnings[], checkedBy}`: an **advisory** plausibility check\n  on the workload *you* submitted (rules + an LLM pass). It never changes the price. **If `verdict` is\n  `suspect`, re-check your extraction against the warnings before trusting the number.**\n- **`comparison`** (the `{supabase}` path only) — the **head-to-head**: `cost.competitor` prices the\n  user's *current fixed Supabase instance billed 24/7* (`cost.competitor.vendor` names it) against the\n  derived *autoscaled* Neon workload (`cost.deltaVsLaunch` = how much more Supabase costs), and\n  `features` summarizes capabilities that are Neon-only (`features.neonOnly`) vs the competitor's\n  (`features.competitorOnly`). Supabase figures are **estimates** carrying `sources[]`/`retrievedAt`\n  (the authoritative pricing pages) — report them as such, and honor `cost.note` (a busy always-on\n  workload narrows the gap; idle widens it).\n\n`GET /api/catalog` documents every shape (agent quickstart). For a formal, machine-readable contract —\nrequest/response schemas for every endpoint, for tooling or codegen — fetch `GET /api/openapi.json`\n(OpenAPI 3.1).\n\n**Neon rates baked in (2026, no monthly minimum):** Launch **$0.106/CU-hr**, Scale **$0.222/CU-hr**;\nstorage **$0.35/GB-mo**; egress **500 GB/project** free on paid then **$0.10/GB** (Free: 5 GB/org, 100\nCU-hr/project, 0.5 GB storage/project). Monthly compute ≈ `avgCu × activeHours × rate`; the always-on\nSupabase baseline for contrast ≈ `peakCu × 744 × rate`. **The gap between those two is the autoscaling +\nscale-to-zero win.**\n\n### B) Compute locally (no POST — nothing leaves the user's environment)\n\nEverything needed to reproduce the estimate yourself. With the mapped workload\n`{peakCu, avgCu, activeHours, storageGb, egressGb}` from Step 1:\n\n**Constants.** 1 CU = 1 vCPU + 4 GB RAM; billing month = 744 h. Autoscaling ceiling **16 CU** (Free 2);\nthe min↔max **range cannot exceed 8 CU** (so reaching a 16-CU peak forces min ≥ 8); scale-to-zero\nsuspends compute after ~5 min idle ($0 while suspended). Rates as above.\n\n**Cost per plan** (rate `r` = 0.106 Launch, 0.222 Scale):\n- `compute = avgCu × activeHours × r`  (measured path: if you have real CU-hours, `compute = cuHours × r`)\n- `storage = storageGb × 0.35`\n- `egress  = max(0, egressGb − 500) × 0.10`   (paid plans; Free egress cap is 5 GB)\n- `total   = round2(compute + storage + egress)`\n\n**Free-tier fit** (headline). Fits Free — **report $0** — when ALL hold: `avgCu × activeHours ≤ 100`\nCU-hr, `storageGb ≤ 0.5`, `egressGb ≤ 5`, and `peakCu ≤ 2`. Otherwise the entry paid plan is **Launch**.\n`recommendation = fitsFree ? {plan:\"free\", monthlyTotal:\"0.00\"} : {plan:\"launch\", monthlyTotal: launchTotal}`.\n\n**Autoscaling baseline (the \"why\").** `withoutAutoscaling = peakCu × 744 × 0.106 + storage + egress` — a\nfixed compute at peak, 24/7. The difference between it and the autoscaled `total` **is** the saving; show\nboth. The whole estimate presupposes autoscaling + scale-to-zero are on.\n\n**Range (inferred inputs).** `avgCu` and `activeHours` are the low-confidence movers. Recompute `total`\nwith each at a plausible low/high bound (e.g. `avgCu` ±~50%; `activeHours` across the activity band) →\nreport **lo–hi**, and name whichever moves the total most as the top mover. Don't present a single\nconfident number when inputs were inferred.\n\n**Self-check vectors** (your local math must match these exactly):\n- `{peakCu 2, avgCu 0.3, activeHours 744, storageGb 8, egressGb 40}` → compute `0.3×744×0.106 = 23.66`,\n  storage `2.80`, egress `0` ⇒ **Launch $26.46**; Scale `0.3×744×0.222 + 2.80 = $52.35`.\n- `{cuHours 92, storageGb 0.4, egressGb 3}` → `92×0.106 + 0.4×0.35 = $9.89`; fits Free (92<100, 0.4<0.5,\n  3<5) ⇒ **recommendation Free $0**.\n\n**Compute the Supabase side locally too** (for the head-to-head). Supabase Pro bills a **fixed instance\n24/7** on an `instance_month` model — no autoscaling, no scale-to-zero:\n- `supabaseTotal = base($25 Pro) + instancePrice − credit($10) + storageOver + egressOver`\n- `instancePrice` (Pro, $/mo): micro 10, small 15, medium 60, **large 110**, xl 210, 2xl 410, 4xl 960, 8xl 1870.\n- `storageOver = max(0, storageGb − 8) × 0.125`; `egressOver = max(0, egressGb − 250) × 0.09` (Pro allowances: 8 GB disk, 250 GB egress included).\n- **Self-check:** large, 8 GB, 40 GB egress → `25 + 110 − 10 + 0 + 0 = ` **$125.00** (matches the server).\n- The **head-to-head** is then `supabaseTotal` (fixed 24/7) vs the Neon `total` (autoscaled) above — the same gap the `{supabase}` estimate returns as `comparison.cost.deltaVsLaunch`.\n\n**Don't want to derive rates by hand?** `GET /api/pricing` returns the full machine-readable rate card\n(both vendors: per-metric rates, instance grids, `sources[]`), and `POST /api/estimate\n{\"vendor\":\"supabase\",\"plan\":\"pro\",\"instance\":\"large\",\"storageGb\":8,\"egressGb\":40}` prices a **known**\ninstance directly (no workload extraction, no data beyond those aggregate numbers).\n\n**What local mode gives up vs POST:** the server's advisory **validation** (rules + LLM plausibility check\non your numbers) doesn't run locally, and cents may differ by rounding (the server uses exact money math).\nIf you want the authoritative figure and a sanity-check on your extracted inputs, POST (option A) — after\nconfirming the payload with the user.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3 — Feature-fit (detect → Neon equivalent / gap)\n\nTwo complementary sources — use both:\n\n- **Capability comparison (data-driven, no input sent):** `GET /api/features` returns the capability\n  matrix — Neon vs every competitor, per-plan (scale-to-zero, autoscaling, PITR, branching, SSO, …).\n  Scope it with `?vendor=`: `?vendor=neon` for Neon's plans alone, `?vendor=supabase` for the\n  Neon-vs-Supabase pair; `?tier=free` compares the **same tier across vendors** (Neon Free vs Supabase\n  Free). The `{supabase}` **estimate response** also embeds a compact `comparison.features` summary. Use\n  these for the plan-level \"what does each vendor give you\" picture.\n- **Project inventory (below):** the matrix says what a *plan* supports; it does **not** know what *this\n  project* actually uses. Inventory the live extensions/schemas to find real migration work and gaps.\n\nInventory what the project actually uses, and size it:\n\n```\nSELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension ORDER BY 1;\nSELECT nspname FROM pg_namespace\n WHERE nspname IN ('auth','storage','realtime','vault','supabase_functions','graphql_public','pgmq','cron','net');\n```\n\n| Supabase feature | Detect | Neon mapping | Caveat / gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Storage** (buckets/BLOBs) | `storage` schema; `select count(*) from storage.buckets` | **Neon Object Storage** (S3-compatible, branch-aware; Beta, us-east-2) | Transfer files via S3 SDK/CLI/rclone |\n| **Auth** (users/MAU) | `auth.users`; MAU from Auth API | **Neon Managed Better Auth** (Beta) | ⚠️ **Password hashes can't migrate** → users re-auth; no phone/SMS or SAML SSO yet |\n| **Realtime** | `realtime`/`_realtime` schema; replication slots | **GAP — no managed realtime** | Re-architect on LISTEN/NOTIFY, Functions, or external (Pusher/Ably) |\n| **Edge Functions** | `supabase_functions` schema; `list_edge_functions` | **Neon Functions** (Node.js 24, Beta) | Port Deno/TS → Node.js; deploy via `neon.ts` / Neon CLI |\n| **Vector** | `pg_extension` name `vector` | **pgvector — 1:1** | Supported every plan (HNSW/IVFFlat) |\n| **Cron** | `pg_extension` name `pg_cron` | **pg_cron — supported** | ⚠️ **Won't fire while scaled to zero** → keep that compute active; `schedule_in_database` unsupported |\n| **Queues** | `pg_extension` name `pgmq`; `pgmq` schema | **GAP/PARTIAL — pgmq not supported** | Reimplement with `SELECT … FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` or external queue |\n| **Data API** | PostgREST at `/rest/v1/` | **Neon Data API** (PostgREST-style, Beta) | Query syntax compatible |\n\nFlag any **gap** feature (Realtime, Queues) as a migration cost item — external service or rework.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4 — Migration checklist (feasibility + effort)\n\n- **Extensions:** cross-check every row of `SELECT extname, extversion FROM pg_extension;` against\n  `neon.com/docs/extensions/pg-extensions` for the target PG version.\n  - *Port cleanly (supported on Neon — verified):* pgvector, pg_cron (compute-active only), postgis\n    (+related), pg_trgm, hstore, btree_gin/gist, citext, pgcrypto, timescaledb (**Apache-2 edition only —\n    no compression/tiered**), and — despite being Supabase-associated — **pg_graphql** (1.5.x) and\n    **pgjwt** (0.2.0) are supported too, so they don't block a restore. `pg_search` is supported on\n    PG16/17 but **not PG18** → use `lakebase_text` there.\n  - *Do NOT transfer / rework (not on Neon):* **pgsodium**, **supabase_vault**, **pg_net**, **wrappers**,\n    **supautils**, **pgmq**. Including these in a dump causes fatal restore failures.\n- **Postgres version:** Neon supports **PG 14–18**; create the Neon project matching (or exceeding) the\n  source major version — no downgrades. (Pick the version when creating the project.)\n- **Data-move path (Neon's Import Assistant does NOT support Supabase):**\n  - Offline (any size): `pg_dump -Fc --schema=public --no-owner --no-acl` from the **unpooled** conn\n    (port 5432, not the Supavisor pooler 6543) → `pg_restore --no-owner --no-acl`. Repeat `--schema=`\n    for each custom user schema.\n  - Low-downtime / large: logical replication, cut over at the end.\n  - Only `public` (+ user schemas) transfer; **never** blind-restore `auth`/`storage`/`realtime`/\n    `vault`/`supabase_functions`/`graphql_public`.\n- **Auth & RLS (per neon.com/docs/auth/migrate/from-supabase):** Neon's Supabase auth migration is an\n  **app-level SDK swap**, not a SQL rewrite — replace `@supabase/supabase-js` with\n  `@neondatabase/neon-js` and find/replace `supabase.` → `client.`; auth method calls and `client.from()`\n  queries stay the same. **User IDs remain UUID** (no FK remapping). **Existing password users cannot\n  migrate** (different hashing) → they re-auth or use OAuth; plan for new accounts. Managed Better Auth\n  users live in `neon_auth.user`; **no** phone/SMS, SAML SSO, or Web3. For RLS, inspect current policies\n  with `SELECT schemaname,tablename,policyname,qual FROM pg_policies;` and re-implement them on Neon\n  (neon.com/docs/guides/row-level-security) — there is **no** `auth.uid()`→`auth.user_id()` or\n  `anon`→`anonymous` rewrite.\n\n---\n\n## Fallback: the two-question rule\n\nIf the crux inputs (**avg utilization**, **activity pattern**, **egress**) can't be measured — no\nManagement-API access, Free tier (no Metrics API), or expired retention — **ask at most two** targeted\nquestions rather than guess:\n\n1. *\"Roughly what fraction of the day is your database actively serving queries vs idle — business-hours\n   only, or 24/7? And do you run background jobs (pg_cron) that need it always on?\"* — sets `activeHours`\n   and whether scale-to-zero even applies.\n2. *\"Typical vs peak CPU/memory on your current Supabase instance (from Observability/Reports), and your\n   monthly egress (from Billing)?\"* — sets `avgCu`/`peakCu`/`egressGb`.\n\nThen estimate with the answers, marked `source: user`, and keep the range prominent.\n\n---\n\n## Honesty guardrail (official Neon tool — credibility > persuasion)\n\n- **Every claim ties to a number** from the estimate or the workload.\n- **Keep the two levers straight:** *autoscaling* (bill avg CU, not provisioned peak) and *scale-to-zero*\n  (bill active hours, not 744) are different arguments.\n- **Show the range and the top movers**, not a single confident number — B-path inputs are inferred.\n- **Say when Neon is NOT cheaper.** A steady, high-utilization, always-on workload gives up both levers;\n  if measured avg CPU is high (>~70%) and load is 24/7, autoscaling savings shrink toward the ~2.4×/50%\n  floor and a fixed instance may win — recommend a fixed Neon compute (scale-to-zero disabled) and\n  compare directly. The ~7.5× figure applies **only** to small/intermittent scale-to-zero workloads — do\n  not stack it with 2.4×.\n- **Flag Beta features** (Object Storage, Functions, Data API, Managed Better Auth) and any **gaps**\n  (Realtime, Queues) as risk/effort.\n\n## Sources\nSupabase: `guides/platform/compute-and-disk`, `guides/telemetry/metrics`, `reference/api`\n(`v1-list-project-addons`, `v1-scrape-project-metrics`), `guides/platform/manage-your-usage/egress`,\nMCP (`github.com/supabase/mcp`). Neon: `pricing`, `docs/introduction/{plans,autoscaling,cost-optimization}`,\n`autoscaling-report` (Dec 2025 telemetry: ~2.4×/50%, ~7.5×), `docs/extensions/pg-extensions`,\n`docs/import/migrate-from-supabase`, `docs/auth/migrate/from-supabase`, `guides/complete-supabase-migration`,\n`docs/data-api/overview`, `docs/storage/overview`.\n"
};
